bodyText:
Selling a rented home in Puerto Rico does not end or shorten the lease unless the lease says so: the buyer takes the property with the tenancy, and the same is true if the landlord dies. If you want the right to end a fixed term when you sell, the lease must reserve it (Sale of the Property: Landlord's Right to End the Lease, optional).

When you sell or hand the property to a new manager:
- (1) tell the tenant in writing, on paper, who the new landlord or manager is and where to pay rent from what date; a tenant who pays you before learning of the transfer is protected, because a debtor who pays the original creditor before learning of an assignment is released;
- (2) deal with the security deposit at closing: you stay liable to return it unless the tenant agrees to look to the buyer (see The Deposit When You Sell);
- (3) a new third-party manager who leases or administers the property for pay needs a Puerto Rico real estate broker's license.

No Puerto Rico statute requires a set form or deadline for that notice, gives the tenant a right to buy, or makes the seller disclose the tenancy to the buyer, though you should. Showings during the lease need justified cause and seven days' notice (see Landlord's Right of Entry). A foreclosure sale follows different rules (see Tenants When the Property Is Foreclosed).
